<h2>Hegel, <em>Introduction to the Philosophy of History</em></h2>

<p>Hegel has made an ambitious claim: that history is not random but rational, that it moves toward the progressive realization of freedom, and that philosophy can comprehend this movement even if the individuals living through it cannot. This is a claim worth testing — both on its own terms and against your own experience of the world.</p>
<p>These two questions are designed to open that examination. Both are genuinely open-ended. There is no correct answer waiting to be discovered; what matters is the quality of your reasoning.</p>

<div>
<p>Question 1 — Is History Going Somewhere?</p>
<p>Hegel argues that history moves toward the realization of freedom — that this is not just something we value, but something that reason itself demands and that history enacts. Do you find this convincing? Looking at the world as you know it, does history appear to you to be progressing toward greater freedom, cycling without direction, or something else? And what is at stake in how you answer that question — does it matter, practically or philosophically, whether history has a direction?</p>
</div>

<div>
<p>Question 2 — World-Historical Individuals</p>
<p>Hegel argues that certain individuals — Napoleon is his example — embody the spirit of their age and move history forward, often without fully understanding their own role. They pursue personal ambition, but their ambition happens to coincide with what the development of Spirit requires. Does this idea hold up? Who, if anyone, might count as a world-historical individual today? And does Hegel’s account leave enough room for individual moral responsibility — or does it risk becoming an excuse for power?</p>
</div>

<div>

<p><strong>Choose one question</strong> and write a substantive initial post (at least two well-developed paragraphs). You don’t need to resolve the question definitively — philosophy rarely works that way. What matters is that you stake out a clear position and give reasons for it.</p>
<p>Once you have submitted your initial post, you will be able to see your classmates’ responses. <strong>Reply to at least two posts</strong> with substantive engagement — ask a question, push back on a premise, offer a counterexample, or extend the argument. Each reply should be at least one solid paragraph. “I agree!” or “Good point!” do not constitute replies.</p>
<p><em>You are welcome to respond to both questions if you wish, but only one initial post is required for credit.</em></p>
</div>

<div>
<p>Academic Integrity</p>
<p>Your posts must represent your own thinking. If you draw on any source other than the assigned reading and the course lecture pages, cite it. Plagiarized posts receive a zero; repeated offenses result in a failing grade for the course.</p>
